<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_gauss3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Application: Charged Sphere</title>

	<assemblage component="implementation">
  <title>Implementation Notes</title>
  <p>
	The activities in this section were designed to be implemented in an interactive studio classroom, after students have completed <xref ref="sec_gauss2"/> and its prerequisite sections.  The activities take about one hour.
	</p>
  </assemblage>

<subsubsection>
	<title>Activities</title>
	
  </subsubsection>

<activity>
	<title>Warm-up</title>
	<p>
		
	</p>
	<ol>
		<li>
			<p>
				Find a flat, rectangular object such as a piece of paper or a small whiteboard.  Use a pen or marker to indicate the area vector for your object.  How many area vectors do you need?
			</p>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				Find an object with multiple flat sides that are not parallel (if you have a sheet of paper, you can fold it into something like a triangular prism).  Again, use a pen or marker to indicate the area vector for your object.  How many area vectors do you need?
			</p>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				Find an object with at least one curved side (if you have a sheet of paper, you can curl it into a cylinder).  Again, use a pen or marker to indicate the area vector for your object.  How many area vectors do you need?
			</p>
		</li>
	</ol>
</activity>
	<activity xml:id="class_charged-sphere">
	<title>Charged Sphere</title>
	
	
	<introduction>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			You want to determine the electric field everywhere in space due to a uniformly charged sphere with charge density <m>\rho</m> and radius <m>R</m>.
		</p>
	</introduction>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Use Gauss's Law to determine the electric field when <m>r \gt R</m>.
			</p>
			<ol>
				<li>
					<p>
						Draw an appropriate Gaussian surface.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						Determine the charge enclosed.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>			
						Determine the flux. 
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						Apply Gauss's Law to find the electric field.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Use Gauss's Law to determine the electric field when <m>r \lt R</m>.
			</p>
			<ol>
				<li>
					<p>
						Draw an appropriate Gaussian surface.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						Determine the charge enclosed.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>			
						Determine the flux. 
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						Apply Gauss's Law to find the electric field.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</statement>
	</task>
	</activity>

<!--NO AI-->
</section>

